{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Encryption, Hashing, and Threshold Schemes: Types and Applications","content":"# Encryption, Hashing, and Threshold Schemes: Types and Applications\n\nIn day-to-day development, \"encryption\" tends to be used as a catch-all: HTTPS, password storage, file checksums, API signing… In reality there are at least several distinct capabilities behind it: **confidentiality (encryption/decryption)**, **integrity (digests)**, **identity and key distribution (asymmetric cryptography)**, and the **threshold control (secret sharing)** commonly seen in HSM scenarios. Separating these categories is more useful than memorizing a pile of algorithm names.\n\nThis post walks through common algorithms grouped as digests, symmetric encryption, asymmetric encryption (RSA / ECC), and threshold secret sharing, covering each from both the **principle** and the **application** side; the last section briefly touches on post-quantum cryptography (PQC). For details on certificates and trust chains, see the earlier post [PKI Architecture and Certificate Trust Chains](/post/en/pkitrustarch).\n\n## First, identify the problem you are solving[ #](#first-identify-the-problem-you-are-solving)\n\n| Goal                             | Typical mechanism                 | In one sentence                                                     |\n| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Confidentiality                  | Symmetric / asymmetric encryption | Without the key, nobody can read the plaintext                      |\n| Integrity                        | Digest (hash)                     | Any modification to the content is detectable                       |\n| Authentication / non-repudiation | Signatures, MAC                   | Prove \"who sent it\", or authenticate a message under a shared key   |\n| Key distribution                 | Asymmetric crypto, key agreement  | Establish a shared key over an untrusted channel                    |\n| Threshold control                | Secret sharing (e.g. Shamir)      | Any set of shares below the threshold cannot reconstruct the secret |\n\nReal systems almost always **combine** these: TLS uses asymmetric crypto for identity and the handshake, then AES to protect the session; Git and software packages use digests for integrity; HSMs use threshold shares to manage master keys and admin tokens. The sections below go through each algorithm family.\n\n## Digest algorithms: turning arbitrary input into a fixed-size fingerprint[ #](#digest-algorithms-turning-arbitrary-input-into-a-fixed-size-fingerprint)\n\n### Principle: a one-way, collision-resistant compression function[ #](#principle-a-one-way-collision-resistant-compression-function)\n\nA digest (hash) algorithm maps input of arbitrary length to output of fixed length; SHA-256, for example, produces 256 bits. Desired properties include:\n\n* **One-wayness**: recovering the input from the digest is infeasible\n* **Collision resistance**: it is hard to find two different inputs with the same digest\n* **Avalanche effect**: flipping one input bit changes the output beyond recognition\n\nCommon members:\n\n| Algorithm                   | Output length   | Status (engineering view)                                         |\n| --------------------------- | --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| MD5                         | 128 bit         | No longer suitable for security use; collision attacks are mature |\n| SHA-1                       | 160 bit         | Not suitable for new systems; long deprecated by browsers/CAs     |\n| SHA-256 / SHA-384 / SHA-512 | 256/384/512 bit | Current mainstream default                                        |\n| SHA-3 (e.g. SHA3-256)       | Variable        | Backup family; structurally different from SHA-2                  |\n| BLAKE2 / BLAKE3             | Variable        | Performance-friendly; used by some newer protocols                |\n\nNote: a digest is **not encryption**. Digest outputs are usually public; they provide no confidentiality, only a \"fingerprint\". When you need integrity plus keyed authentication, use **HMAC** (e.g. HMAC-SHA256) rather than a bare hash of a password or of API parameters.\n\nPassword storage typically uses **slow hashes / key derivation functions**: bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, PBKDF2, and so on. Their goal is to be deliberately slow and resist brute force, which is a different problem from storing a plain SHA-256 hash of the password.\n\n### Applications: verification, signature preprocessing, key derivation[ #](#applications-verification-signature-preprocessing-key-derivation)\n\n* **Integrity checks**: ISO images, software packages, object storage ETag / Content-MD5 (legacy), Git object IDs\n* **Input to digital signatures**: RSA / ECDSA usually sign the message digest, not the full plaintext\n* **Certificates and PKI**: certificate fingerprints, OCSP / CRL identifiers; algorithm choice affects the whole trust chain (see the PKI post)\n* **TLS / protocols**: hashing the handshake transcript, key derivation such as HKDF\n* **Deduplication and indexing**: content-addressable storage (CAS) uses hashes as keys; here uniqueness and performance matter most, and the collision-resistance requirement depends on the scenario\n\nRecommendation: new systems should default to **SHA-256** (or SHA-384/512 as the protocol requires); do not use MD5/SHA-1 for anything security-related. Legacy systems that show \"SHA1 fingerprints\" should be flagged as tech debt with a migration plan.\n\n## AES: the workhorse of symmetric encryption[ #](#aes-the-workhorse-of-symmetric-encryption)\n\n### Principle: one shared key, the same algorithm for encryption and decryption[ #](#principle-one-shared-key-the-same-algorithm-for-encryption-and-decryption)\n\nIn symmetric encryption, encryption and decryption use **the same key**. AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) is a block cipher: plaintext is processed in fixed 128-bit blocks, with a key length of 128, 192, or 256 bits.\n\nThe algorithm alone is not enough; you also need to pick a **mode of operation**:\n\n* **GCM**: the most common authenticated encryption (AEAD) mode today, providing both confidentiality and integrity\n* **CBC** and others: still found in older systems; they need a separate MAC and carry higher misuse risk\n* **ECB**: really only good as a textbook counterexample; identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks\n\nThe security boundary is clear: **key secrecy ≈ security**. A public algorithm and open-source implementation do not weaken security; what really hurts is key leakage, weak randomness, the wrong mode, or plaintext keys hardcoded in source.\n\nCompared with asymmetric crypto, AES is **fast and suited to large volumes of data**; its weakness is that both parties must first share the key securely.\n\n### Applications: disks, TLS sessions, application-level sealing[ #](#applications-disks-tls-sessions-application-level-sealing)\n\n* **Full-disk / volume encryption**: BitLocker, FileVault, LUKS, etc. commonly use AES underneath\n* **TLS record layer**: after the handshake, application data is mostly protected by AES-GCM (or ChaCha20-Poly1305)\n* **Application layer**: database field encryption, server-side encryption in object storage, sealing message payloads in middleware\n* **Tokens and local storage**: some frameworks wrap sessions or sensitive config with AES (the master key still needs proper management)\n\nIn practice the hard part is rarely \"choosing AES\"; it is **where the key comes from, where it is stored, how it is rotated, and who is allowed to decrypt**. Establishing a shared key across a network for the first time is where asymmetric crypto or key-agreement protocols come in.\n\n## RSA: the classic asymmetric scheme[ #](#rsa-the-classic-asymmetric-scheme)\n\n### Principle: the hardness of factoring large integers[ #](#principle-the-hardness-of-factoring-large-integers)\n\nAsymmetric schemes use a **key pair**: the public key can be published, while the private key is kept only by its owner. RSA's security rests on the difficulty of factoring large integers: choose large primes p and q, set the modulus n=p⋅q, then choose a public exponent e and private exponent d such that ed≡1(modφ(n)). The public key is (n,e), and the private key contains d (implementations usually also store p, q, and other parameters for speedups).\n\nDo not confuse its two main uses:\n\n* **Encryption**: encrypt with the public key → decrypt with the private key (usually to wrap a small symmetric key, not to encrypt large files directly)\n* **Signing**: sign with the private key → verify with the public key (proves possession of the private key and that the content was not tampered with; usually signs a digest)\n\nRSA operations are modular exponentiations, **more than an order of magnitude slower** than AES, with larger keys. New systems today typically start at **2048 bits**, with **3072** and above for long-term or high-assurance needs. Using RSA to \"encrypt an entire disk\" is both slow and unnecessary; the standard approach is **RSA/ECC carries the key, AES carries the data** (hybrid encryption).\n\nPadding is not optional: use **OAEP** for encryption and **PSS** for signatures (or whatever the protocol specifies). Raw RSA or misuse of the old PKCS#1 v1.5 has been a breeding ground for historical vulnerabilities.\n\n### Applications: certificates, signatures, key wrapping[ #](#applications-certificates-signatures-key-wrapping)\n\n* **TLS / HTTPS**: RSA was once used for key exchange; in modern cipher suites RSA certificates mainly serve the **signing** role, while key agreement is usually done with ECDHE\n* **Certificates and PKI**: many server certificates and code-signing certificates still carry RSA public keys\n* **Code signing, document signing, S/MIME email**: the private key proves the publisher's identity\n* **JWT / JWS**, etc.: algorithms like `RS256` mean \"SHA-256 + RSA signature\"\n\nCommon pitfalls: keys that are too short, private keys committed to repositories, using one key pair for both encryption and signing with a muddled lifecycle, and home-made \"encryption padding\". If a system or mature library offers a high-level API, do not assemble modular exponentiation yourself.\n\n## ECC: shorter keys, equivalent strength[ #](#ecc-shorter-keys-equivalent-strength)\n\n### Principle: the elliptic curve discrete logarithm[ #](#principle-the-elliptic-curve-discrete-logarithm)\n\nElliptic curve cryptography (ECC) is built on the **discrete logarithm problem** over the group of points on a curve: given a base point G and the scalar multiple kG, recovering the scalar k is hard. The core operation is scalar multiplication of points, and for the same security level, keys are much shorter than RSA's; a curve with roughly **256-bit** strength is broadly comparable to RSA with thousands of bits.\n\nCommon curves and their uses (briefly):\n\n* **NIST P-256** and similar: very common in TLS and the certificate ecosystem\n* **X25519**: key agreement (a Diffie–Hellman variant)\n* **Ed25519**: signatures (EdDSA), with a simple implementation and good performance\n\nShorter keys mean smaller certificates and faster handshakes, which particularly helps mobile clients and high-concurrency TLS termination. The cost is that curves and parameters must come from a trusted set; \"inventing your own curve\" is usually a preview of an incident.\n\n### Applications: the default choice for modern TLS and mobile[ #](#applications-the-default-choice-for-modern-tls-and-mobile)\n\n* **ECDHE**: key agreement with forward secrecy in TLS (leaking a session key does not expose past traffic)\n* **ECDSA / EdDSA**: certificate signatures, JWT (e.g. `ES256`), SSH host and user keys\n* **Trade-off with RSA**: new systems should prefer ECC (performance, size); keep RSA when you must support old clients or existing RSA certificate chains\n\nThe \"public key algorithm\" field in a certificate is exactly where RSA / ECC show up; for how relying parties validate the full chain, see the [PKI post](/post/en/pkitrustarch).\n\n## Threshold schemes and secret sharing: Shamir and others[ #](#threshold-schemes-and-secret-sharing-shamir-and-others)\n\nWhat is commonly called **Shamir's Secret Sharing (SSS)** was proposed by Adi Shamir. It is **not** an encryption algorithm like AES, nor a public-key algorithm like RSA/ECC; it is a **secret sharing** scheme. With the constraint \"at least t shares are needed to reconstruct\", it is often classified under the broader category of **threshold cryptography / threshold schemes**.\n\nDo not mix up neighboring concepts:\n\n| Name                                        | What it solves                                                                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Secret sharing (e.g. Shamir)                | Split a secret into n shares; any t shares reconstruct it, fewer than t reveal nothing                       |\n| Threshold signatures / threshold decryption | Multiple private key shares cooperate to sign or decrypt; the full private key need never exist in one place |\n| Plain backup copies                         | Every copy is the full key; any single leak compromises everything, with no threshold protection             |\n\n### Principle: t-of-n and polynomial interpolation[ #](#principle-t-of-n-and-polynomial-interpolation)\n\nThe intuition behind Shamir's scheme: over a finite field, construct a random polynomial of degree t−1 and put the secret in the constant term (or at an agreed evaluation point); give each participant one point on the curve (a share). Any **t** points uniquely determine the polynomial and therefore recover the secret; **fewer than t points** yield no useful information about the secret in the information-theoretic sense.\n\nThe engineering parameter is written as **t-of-n** (or m-of-n): for example 3-of-5 means 5 admin shares are issued, any 3 can be combined, and 2 cannot. The threshold provides both:\n\n* **Resistance to a single bad actor**: no one person can obtain the full master key / admin token\n* **Resistance to partial loss**: a few shares can be lost and recovery is still possible (as long as t remain)\n\nThe shares themselves must still be handled as sensitive material (separate people, locations, media); the algorithm only guarantees \"below the threshold, nothing combines\", and does not replace physical and procedural controls.\n\n### Applications: HSM administration and key ceremonies[ #](#applications-hsm-administration-and-key-ceremonies)\n\nThe most typical use is in **HSMs** and high-assurance key management:\n\n* **Threshold tokens / admin card distribution**: during device initialization or a key ceremony, the master key, unseal key, or admin privileges are split across multiple smart cards / tokens handed to several administrators; booting, unsealing, and critical operations require a quorum\n* **Root keys and CA ceremonies**: the root private key or seed is not held by any single person, reducing insider threat and single-person error\n* **System unsealing**: for example, some key management systems split the master key with Shamir, and starting the cluster requires several operators to unseal it together\n\nRelationship to AES/RSA: a threshold scheme governs **\"who is allowed to assemble that critical key\"**; once assembled, business data is still usually encrypted with AES and signed with RSA/ECC. A common combination in HSMs is: **business keys stay inside the device**, while **administrative privileges are controlled by threshold tokens for export, unsealing, and dual-control review**.\n\n## PQC: algorithm families for the quantum era[ #](#pqc-algorithm-families-for-the-quantum-era)\n\n### Principle: switch to a different set of \"hard problems\"[ #](#principle-switch-to-a-different-set-of-hard-problems)\n\nIf large-scale fault-tolerant quantum computers become available, Shor's algorithm would seriously threaten the mathematical problems that **RSA and ECC** depend on. Symmetric algorithms and hashes are comparatively robust: the main countermeasure is **longer keys / outputs** (e.g. AES-256, longer digests), not replacing the whole family.\n\nPost-quantum cryptography (PQC) uses problems believed to be quantum-resistant (lattices, hash-based signatures, etc.). NIST has been driving standardization, and the directions you commonly hear about in engineering include:\n\n* **ML-KEM** (Kyber): a key encapsulation mechanism (KEM), used to negotiate/wrap symmetric keys\n* **ML-DSA** (Dilithium) and others: digital signatures\n\nThe transition period commonly uses **hybrid mode**: a classical algorithm (such as X25519) runs alongside a PQC KEM, and the session is only at risk if both are broken, which reduces the risk of \"betting everything on one scheme before the standards settle\".\n\n### Applications: standards rollout and migration reality[ #](#applications-standards-rollout-and-migration-reality)\n\n* **TLS / certificates**: browsers and some CDNs and cloud providers are already experimenting with or gradually rolling out hybrid key exchange; PQC signatures and dual certificate chains on the certificate side are still evolving\n* **Engineering cost**: public keys and ciphertexts are often larger, increasing handshake size and CPU; protocols, hardware, HSMs, and compliance checklists all need to change\n* **Pragmatic judgment**: secrets with very long retention (\"harvest now, decrypt later\") should be assessed first; typical web workloads can simply follow mainstream protocols and vendor roadmaps, with no need to implement PQC primitives yourself\n\nPQC is a **long-term migration problem**, not a switch that replaces RSA everywhere tomorrow. Retiring MD5/SHA-1 for digests, using AEAD for symmetric encryption, and getting asymmetric key lengths and padding right yield more certain returns first.\n\n## Comparison and selection: what to use where[ #](#comparison-and-selection-what-to-use-where)\n\n| Category                   | Representative           | Good at                                                       | Main cost                                            |\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| Digest                     | SHA-256                  | Integrity, signature preprocessing, derivation                | No confidentiality; never use MD5/SHA-1 for security |\n| MAC                        | HMAC-SHA256              | Message authentication under a shared key                     | Requires a pre-shared key                            |\n| Symmetric encryption       | AES-GCM                  | Confidentiality + integrity for bulk data                     | Key distribution and custody                         |\n| Asymmetric (RSA)           | RSA-OAEP / RSA-PSS       | Widely compatible key wrapping and signatures                 | Slow, large keys                                     |\n| Asymmetric (ECC)           | X25519 / Ed25519 / P-256 | Cost-effective key agreement and signatures                   | Choice of curve and ecosystem                        |\n| Threshold / secret sharing | Shamir t-of-n            | Multi-party custody, no single point, fault-tolerant recovery | Process and share custody overhead                   |\n| PQC                        | ML-KEM / ML-DSA, etc.    | Long-term quantum-resistant security                          | Size and ecosystem maturity                          |\n\nA typical pipeline fits in one sentence:\n\n**Digests preserve integrity → asymmetric crypto identifies parties / delivers keys → symmetric crypto protects bulk data; critical master keys can be held in threshold shares.**\n\nTake TLS: the certificate (RSA/ECC public key + signature) proves the server's identity → ECDHE (or hybrid PQC) negotiates the session key → AES-GCM encrypts application data, with SHA-family hashes used repeatedly throughout. On the HSM side there is a separate administrative chain: Shamir threshold tokens → unseal/authorize → symmetric or asymmetric operations for business inside the device.\n\n## Summary[ #](#summary)\n\n1. **Digests** answer \"has this been modified?\"; **symmetric encryption** answers \"how do we keep large amounts of data confidential?\"; **asymmetric crypto** answers \"how are keys distributed and identities proven?\"; **threshold secret sharing** (e.g. Shamir) answers \"how do we prevent any one person from holding the complete secret?\".\n2. A reasonable default for new systems is roughly: SHA-256 (or as the protocol specifies), AES-GCM, and ECC (key agreement/signatures); RSA appears mostly for compatibility and existing certificates; HSM administration commonly uses t-of-n shares.\n3. **PQC** is worth tracking, but get the classical algorithms right first: retire MD5/SHA-1, use AEAD, and use sufficient key lengths with correct padding.\n","locale":"en","slug":"cryptoandhashintro"}}